/**
 * TRIAL798 campaign email — deliberately mirrors the marketing popup's own
 * dark, high-energy visual identity (PromoTrialPopup.tsx / its CSS in
 * app/globals.css), per an explicit request to make the email "look like the
 * popup" rather than use the standard light transactional shell
 * (lib/email-render.ts's wrapEmail).
 *
 * REAL TENSION, worth restating here for whoever reads this next: the win-back
 * email (lib/email-templates.ts's `deployWinback`) documents a real 2026-08-24
 * finding — a loud subject line + a big coupon graphic + a prominent
 * "Unsubscribe" footer read to Gmail as a promotional blast and got routed to
 * Promotions, with ~0% opens even from engaged recipients. This template is
 * intentionally the OTHER direction (dark theme, poster art, a big gradient
 * CTA) because that's what was asked for — it is NOT the deliverability-safe
 * default, and that tradeoff should be a conscious choice at send time, not
 * an accident of "it matched the popup." The one place this template does NOT
 * copy the popup: no live countdown. A popup can run a real JS/server timer;
 * a static HTML email sent to 654 people at once cannot, and faking one (a
 * GIF timer, say) would be the same fabricated-urgency problem already
 * flagged and deliberately avoided for the popup's OWN scarcity ticker — see
 * PromoTrialPopup.tsx's SPOTS_START comment. This uses the coupon's real,
 * honest, SHARED deadline (Coupon.expiresAt / CAMPAIGN_END) instead, the same
 * number every recipient would see if they opened the email at a different
 * moment.
 *
 * Table-based, fully inline-styled — Gmail/Outlook/Apple Mail strip <style>
 * and don't support flexbox/grid/backdrop-filter/CSS transforms, same
 * constraints as lib/email-render.ts.
 */

